Seropositive myasthenia gravis severity and antibodies pro ﬁle
A. Alkhotani a, N. Alrishi b, A. Alkhotani c
aUMM Alqura University, Medicine, Makakh, Saudi Arabia
bKING Abdulla Edical City, Neurology, Makkah, Saudi Arabia
cUm Alqura University, Pathology, Makkah, Saudi Arabia
Myasthenia Gravis (MG) is an acquired autoimmune disease of
the neuromuscular junction. Patient with MG present with fatigable
muscle weakness. It is resulted from impairment of neuromuscular
transmission. The majority of MG patients have a positive antibodies.
The most common antibody is antibodies against acetyl choline
receptors (AChR) and less commonly antibodies to muscle speci ﬁc
kinase antibodies (MUSK) and antibodies against low density
lipoprotein receptor related protein 4 (LRP4).
This study aimed to correlate the severity of MG to their anti
AChR and or anti-MUSK antibody status. We reviewed the MG
patients in King Abdullah Medical City from period 2011 to 2019. Out
of the total 27 MG patients, 16 were seropositivity. The severity was
correlated with antibodies status. We found 87.5% of those patients
to be positive to AChR antibodies only while 12.5% were positive to
both Anti-AChR and Anti-MuSK. Thymoma was found in 21% of
single seropositivity and in 100% with dual seropositivity. Crises
were was seen in 21% of single seropositivity patients while 100% in
patients with dual seropositivity. Patients with dual seropositivity
have more aggressive disease and required prolonged ICU admis-
sions. Our Observational study concluded that having dual seropos-
itivity may carry a more aggressive course of the MG and an
association to Thymoma. More clinical trials are needed to con ﬁrm
the severity of MG presentation and dual seropositivity.
